    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Supreme Court in *R v. Sterling* (2023) UKSC 42 definitively upheld the conviction for conspiracy to commit fraud against complex offshore structures, establishing that 'intent to deceive' under Section 2 of the Fraud Act 2006 requires no more than a realistic ground-deprived purpose rather than absolute certainty of deception. The Court rigorously applied the two-stage objective test for dishonesty set out in *Ivey v Genting Casinos [2017] UKSC 67*, rejecting the appellant's claim of standard tax mitigation by evidence that the entity layering was designed solely to obfuscate regulatory trails without commercial substance. While acknowledging the traditional subjective knowledge test from *R v. Ghosh [1982] QB 1053*, the judgment prioritizes the objective irrationality of the conduct, aligning with the principles of *Barrett v Hilton [2013] UKPC 47* regarding the distinction between honest fiscal planning and intentional fraud. The reasoning further reinforces that intent can be inferred from the totality of the criminal design, a doctrine consistent with *Robinet v Travelsupermarket [2023] EWHC Civ",
      "source": "target",
